# Where the pins of deploy/chart/tests/ratelimit-e2e/ come from, and what each was verified against. `ratelimit-e2e.sh` reads the `key=value` lines (the comments
# are for people), downloads the Traefik chart from its URL and refuses one whose SHA-256 is not the pinned one. The workflow
# (.github/workflows/ratelimit-e2e.yml) reads `kind_node_image` from here and pins the three tools it installs (kind, kubectl, helm) with their checksums in its
# own `env`, as backup-e2e.yml does (deploy/chart/tests/backup-e2e/UPSTREAM, whose node image this is). Move a pin and its comment together.
#
# NOTHING OF THE CLUSTER HALF HAS RUN where this was written (no kind, no kubectl): the pins are checked against their sources, the script against shellcheck and
# dash, the chart's objects against helm and the vendored Middleware schema, and the SCENARIO (steps 2 to 4 of the script, its assertions unchanged) against
# the pinned Traefik image itself, run with docker as a file-provider Traefik beside the pinned whoami and three pinned curl containers on one bridge network
# (the Middleware's fields written into Traefik's file configuration, which reads the same struct as the Kubernetes CRD provider). That run did not exercise
# Traefik's Kubernetes providers, the annotation `router.middlewares` or the Ingress, which is what the first run of the job `ratelimit-e2e` is for.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-10-28, from the sources below (anonymous requests through the sandbox's proxy):
#
#   * Traefik chart 42.1.3, appVersion v3.7.16: https://traefik.github.io/charts/index.yaml lists it as the newest `traefik` release (created 2026-10-22) with the
#     digest below, and the downloaded file has that SHA-256. `kubeVersion` >=1.30 (the node image below is 1.35). The chart installs its CRDs from its `crds/`
#     directory with `helm install` (the Middleware among them, `traefik.io_middlewares.yaml`), the IngressClass `traefik` for a release called `traefik`, and the
#     Service `traefik` in the release's namespace (ports 80 and 443 for the entrypoints web and websecure; `service.spec.type: ClusterIP` is set here). Its default
#     `providers.kubernetesCRD` and `providers.kubernetesIngress` are on, and `crossProviderNamespaces` is empty, which renders no flag: every namespace may
#     name a Middleware from an Ingress. `ports.<entrypoint>.forwardedHeaders.trustedIPs` becomes `--entryPoints.<name>.forwardedHeaders.trustedIPs=` (templates/_podtemplate.tpl).
#   * The Traefik image: docker.io/traefik:v3.7.16, index digest below (Docker Hub answered 429 to the anonymous token request, mirror.gcr.io answered the tag and the
#     digest, and a pull by digest from it works). The chart's `image.digest` replaces the tag; `versionOverride` tells its version checks which Traefik that is.
#   * traefik/whoami:v1.11.0, index digest below (mirror.gcr.io answered the tag; the digest pulls). curlimages/curl:8.22.0, index digest below (the tag is the one
#     the docker-compose-patterns skill's sidecar names; the digest pulls).
#   * kind v0.33.0, kubectl v1.35.9 and helm v3.19.0 and the node image kindest/node:v1.35.0: the pins of backup-e2e (their checksums were recomputed on 2026-10-17
#     from kind's .sha256sum, dl.k8s.io's .sha256 and get.helm.sh's .sha256sum; this file does not recompute them).
#   * The facts the assertions rest on are from Traefik's own source at v3.7.15 (raw.githubusercontent.com/traefik/traefik/v3.7.15), and the scenario above ran them:
#       - pkg/middlewares/ratelimiter/rate_limiter.go: a token bucket per source, `burst` tokens, refilled at average/period; a request that finds the bucket empty is
#         refused with 429 and `Retry-After` and `X-Retry-In` when the wait would exceed half the interval of a token (a cap of 500 ms below one request a second),
#         else delayed; the source of a request that has none is "" and is a bucket like any other; no special case for it;
#       - pkg/ip/strategy.go: depth 0 (no `excludedIPs`) is the host part of the peer's address (RemoteAddr); depth N is the N-th entry from the right of the FIRST
#         `X-Forwarded-For` header line, and "" when the header has fewer entries;
#       - pkg/middlewares/forwardedheaders/forwarded_header.go: a peer that is not in `trustedIPs` has its X-Forwarded-* headers deleted at the entrypoint, before any
#         middleware; the peer's own address is not appended there (the proxy appends it later, after the middlewares), so a middleware sees the header as the client
#         (or the trusted proxy in front) sent it;
#       - the namespaced reference of an Ingress to a Middleware of the Kubernetes CRD provider is `<namespace>-<name>@kubernetescrd`
#         (doc.traefik.io, "Kubernetes providers: Provider Namespace"), and with `providers.kubernetesCRD.safeNaming` (v3.7.11 and later) it is
#         `<namespace>_<name>@kubernetescrd`: "A reference left in the legacy form is not resolved, and the routers using it are not created" (docs/content/migrate/v3.md at v3.7.15).
#
